  <h1>My Story</h1>

  <h2>How I Started Programming</h2>
  <p>
    It started in 7th grade, when I got my first MacBook. I built my first
    command-line calculator in C++. Back then I dreamed of making a video game
    inspired by the film Chappie. A (slightly too) ambitious project for a
    middle-schooler, but it showed me I already had a maker’s mind. In middle
    school I found Scratch, then in 10th grade I took the ICN option (computing
    and digital creation), where I learned the basics of Python.
  </p>

  <h2>When the Passion Kicked In</h2>
  <p>
    In 11th grade I picked the NSI specialty (digital and computer science).
    That’s where everything really began. I built a meal recommendation app in
    JavaScript, then rewrote it several times in Vue 2 to improve its
    architecture. The COVID lockdown gave me free time, and I spent it levelling
    up. That’s when I made one of the most important choices of my life: joining
    Hiberfile, an open source alternative to WeTransfer, as lead developer. That
    project taught me both the front end and the back end, with a few hundred
    users to look after.
  </p>

  <h2>My Time at School</h2>
  <p>
    After high school I joined HETIC, a school known for development. I went for
    a bachelor’s in data and AI rather than web, because I wanted to explore new
    ground. That choice turned out well: data and AI now sit at the heart of
    technology.
    <br />At HETIC I chose to focus on the structure of my projects and clean
    code, while the program aimed to teach us to ship value fast. Today, doing a
    master’s at EEMI, I have done the opposite: the program is built to teach us
    how to architect projects, and I have focused on shipping value fast. The
    result? I now handle both key sides of development: a scalable architecture
    and efficient delivery. That mix lets me make the best trade-offs for what
    you need, whether that is a quick prototype or something built to last.
  </p>

  <h2>My Technical World</h2>
  <p>
    Today I would call myself a very general developer. I have explored a lot of
    languages and technologies over the years, which lets me adapt to almost any
    project. Whether it is TypeScript, Golang, React Native or plenty of other
    tools, I am comfortable diving into new ecosystems and finding the best fit.
    My goal? Stay close to what projects actually need while I keep exploring
    and learning.
  </p>

  <a href="/contact" class="btn mt-4" data-astro-prefetch>Contact Me</a>
</Layout>
